<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|tmdoc|english>>

<\body>
  <tmdoc-title|Language servers for source code>

  When you edit a source file (a <c++>, <name|Python>, <name|Java>... file
  opened in <TeXmacs>), <TeXmacs> can ask a <em|language server> about it.
  A language server is an external program, the same one which other
  editors use through the <em|Language Server Protocol> (<abbr|LSP>), which
  analyzes the code of a whole project. With a server, <TeXmacs> underlines
  the errors and warnings of the file while you type, completes names,
  shows information about the symbol at the cursor and jumps to its
  definition.

  Source files are shown in a monospaced font, <name|Inconsolata> by
  default; another one can be chosen in <menu|Edit|Preferences|Other|Font of
  source code> (the change reaches the files which are open). This font is
  the one of source files only: the code in a document keeps the fonts of
  the document.

  <paragraph*|Installing language servers>

  <TeXmacs> does not include any language server: you have to install the
  ones you need, so that their programs can be found in your
  <verbatim|PATH>. For each format of source files, <TeXmacs> knows the
  following servers and uses the first one which is installed:

  <\big-table|<tabular|<tformat|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|font-series|bold>|<table|<row|<cell|Format>|<cell|Command
  of the server>>|<row|<cell|C (<verbatim|c>), <c++>
  (<verbatim|cpp>)>|<cell|<verbatim|clangd>>>|<row|<cell|<name|Python>
  (<verbatim|python>)>|<cell|<verbatim|pylsp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<verbatim|pyright-langserver
  --stdio>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<verbatim|basedpyright-langserver
  --stdio>>>|<row|<cell|<name|JavaScript>
  (<verbatim|javascript>)>|<cell|<verbatim|typescript-language-server
  --stdio>>>|<row|<cell|<name|JSON> (<verbatim|json>)>|<cell|<verbatim|vscode-json-language-server
  --stdio>>>|<row|<cell|<name|Java> (<verbatim|java>)>|<cell|<verbatim|jdtls>>>|<row|<cell|<name|Scala>
  (<verbatim|scala>)>|<cell|<verbatim|metals>>>|<row|<cell|<name|Julia>
  (<verbatim|julia>)>|<cell|<verbatim|julia-language-server>>>>>>>
    The language servers known by <TeXmacs>.
  </big-table>

  For instance, <verbatim|clangd> is part of the <name|LLVM> tools (on
  <name|Linux>, it is usually in a package <verbatim|clangd>), and
  <verbatim|pylsp> is installed with <verbatim|pip install
  python-lsp-server>.

  Files with the suffixes <verbatim|.c> and <verbatim|.h> have their own
  format \PC source code\Q (<verbatim|c>), with the syntax highlighting
  of C, and are followed by <verbatim|clangd> as C files. Files of <c++>
  (<verbatim|.cpp>, <verbatim|.hpp>...) keep the format <verbatim|cpp>.

  <paragraph*|Following source files>

  As long as <menu|Tools|Language servers|Follow source files> is checked
  (the default), a source file whose format has an installed server is
  <em|followed> automatically: when it is loaded and after each pause in
  your typing, its text is given to the server; when it is saved, the
  server is told so, and when its buffer is closed (or saved under another
  name), the server forgets it. A server is started the first time a file
  needs it, and then runs as long as <TeXmacs>. Unchecking the menu entry
  stops all the servers; when it is checked again, they are started again
  as the files need them.

  The keys described below work in the files which a server follows;
  elsewhere, <key|F12> and <key|S-F12> keep their usual meaning (in
  presentations).

  The setting is the preference <verbatim|lsp> (<verbatim|on> or
  <verbatim|off>). Only files on disk are followed. The other preferences of
  the servers are <verbatim|lsp:mouse hover>, <verbatim|lsp:highlight>,
  <verbatim|lsp:semantic colors> and <verbatim|lsp:inlay hints> (see
  below).

  <paragraph*|Projects>

  A server is started for a language and a <em|project>, the directory of
  which it analyzes the files together. The project of a file is the
  nearest directory, starting from the directory of the file and going up,
  which contains one of the files or directories

  <\verbatim-code>
    .git \ compile_commands.json \ compile_flags.txt \ pyproject.toml
    \ setup.py

    package.json \ Cargo.toml \ go.mod \ build.sbt \ pom.xml
  </verbatim-code>

  If there is none, the project is the directory of the file. Files of the
  same language in the same project share one server. A <c++> server like
  <verbatim|clangd> needs to know how the files are compiled: give it a
  <verbatim|compile_commands.json> (generated by <name|CMake> with
  <verbatim|-DCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON>) or a
  <verbatim|compile_flags.txt> with one compiler option per line, at the top
  of the project.

  <paragraph*|How the servers are run>

  A server is a program on your computer, which <TeXmacs> starts itself
  and with which it communicates through the standard input and output of
  the program. There is one server for each language and each project: it
  is started the first time a file of that language and that project is
  opened, the other files of the project then share it, and a file of
  another project gets a server of its own. When it starts, a server is
  told the directory of its project (the <verbatim|rootUri> and the
  <verbatim|workspaceFolders> of the protocol), which is how
  <verbatim|clangd>, for instance, finds the <verbatim|compile_commands.json>
  of the project.

  <TeXmacs> does not send options of its own to the servers: there are no
  <verbatim|initializationOptions>, and a server which asks for its
  settings (<verbatim|workspace/configuration>) is answered that there are
  none. Each server is configured with its own files, as with other editors, for
  instance a <verbatim|.clangd> file for <verbatim|clangd> or the
  <verbatim|pyproject.toml> of a <name|Python> project for
  <verbatim|pylsp> and <verbatim|pyright>. Servers which run on another
  computer, reached through a network connection, are not supported yet.

  A server which does not answer within two minutes when it is started is
  ended, with a line in its log (see <menu|Server log> below).

  <paragraph*|Choosing the servers>

  The servers of a format can be changed in your personal initialization
  file <verbatim|~/.TeXmacs/progs/my-init-texmacs.scm>. The commands are
  tried in order, and the first whose program is found is used. For
  instance, to use <verbatim|pyright> for <name|Python> files:

  <\scm-code>
    (use-modules (lsp lsp-client))

    (lsp-server-set! "python" '("pyright-langserver --stdio"))
  </scm-code>

  A command may also start with the full path of the program (a path
  without spaces), and may be followed by its options, separated by
  spaces.

  The language of a file is its format (<verbatim|cpp>, <verbatim|c>,
  <verbatim|python>...) or, for a buffer in program mode, its programming
  language. A server can be given in the same way for another language,
  but only once <TeXmacs> has a format for its files: there is none for
  <name|Rust>, for instance, and one has to be defined first (by a
  plugin). With a format <verbatim|rust>, the server would be given by

  <\scm-code>
    (lsp-server-set! "rust" '("rust-analyzer"))
  </scm-code>

  <paragraph*|Diagnostics>

  The errors and warnings which the server finds (its <em|diagnostics>) are
  underlined in the file: errors in red, warnings in orange, information
  and hints in blue. When the cursor is in an underlined part, its message
  is shown on the status bar. <key|F8> moves the cursor to the next
  diagnostic, <key|S-F8> to the previous one (both start again at the other
  end of the file).

  The diagnostics are those of the text which the server was last given.
  While you type, the underlines disappear; when you pause, those which
  are not in the lines you changed come back, moved with the text, until
  the server sends new diagnostics a moment later.

  <paragraph*|Completion>

  In a followed file, <key|tab> after a word, or after <verbatim|.>,
  <verbatim|-\<gtr\>> or <verbatim|::>, asks the server for the
  completions at the cursor. The answer comes asynchronously, and is
  applied only if the cursor has not moved meanwhile: the first completion
  is inserted and, if there are others, they are listed on the status bar
  and further presses of <key|tab> (or <key|S-tab>) cycle through them, as
  for the other completions of <TeXmacs>. If the server proposes nothing,
  the editor's own completion is tried instead.

  <paragraph*|Information and definitions>

  <key|S-F12> shows what the server says about the symbol at the cursor
  (its type, its documentation...) in a balloon at the cursor, which is
  closed when the cursor moves. <key|F12> goes to the definition of the
  symbol at the cursor, opening its file if needed. On keyboards of
  <name|Mac> computers, the function keys may have to be pressed together
  with <key|fn>.

  <paragraph*|The menu>

  These commands are also in <menu|Tools|Language servers>:

  <\description>
    <item*|<menu|Follow source files>>Whether the servers follow the source
    files (see above).

    <item*|<menu|Find references>, <menu|Outline>, <menu|Find symbol in
    project...>, <menu|Calls>>As <key|std U>, <key|std O>, <key|std P> and
    <key|std H> (see below).

    <item*|<menu|Go to definition>, <menu|Information at cursor>,
    <menu|Complete>>As <key|F12>, <key|S-F12> and <key|tab>.

    <item*|<menu|Next diagnostic>, <menu|Previous diagnostic>>As <key|F8>
    and <key|S-F8>.

    <item*|<menu|List of diagnostics>>As <key|std M> (see below).

    <item*|<menu|Format>, <menu|Rename symbol...>, <menu|Quick fix>>As
    <key|std F>, <key|std E> and <key|std .> (see below).

    <item*|<menu|Information on mouse rest>>Whether the information about a
    symbol is shown when the mouse rests on it (see below).

    <item*|<menu|Fold block>, <menu|Unfold block>, <menu|Fold all>,
    <menu|Unfold all>>See \Pfolding\Q below; the first two are as
    <key|std (> and <key|std )>.

    <item*|<menu|Highlight the symbol at the cursor>>Whether the uses of
    the symbol at the cursor are highlighted (see below).

    <item*|<menu|Colours of the server>, <menu|Inlay hints>>Whether the
    names are coloured by their kinds, and whether the hints are shown (see
    below).

    <item*|<menu|Restart server>>Stops the server of the current file (if it
    runs) and starts it again; the files which are open are given to it
    again. This also starts a server which has ended or which was stopped,
    and looks again for the project of each file (after a
    <verbatim|git init>, or once a <verbatim|compile_commands.json> has
    been made).

    <item*|<menu|Server log>>Opens a page with what the server of the
    current file has written: its messages, the errors it printed and those
    of the communication. The page is updated while it stays open.

    <item*|<menu|Stop all servers>>Stops all the language servers. They stay
    stopped, also when you continue to edit, until you use <menu|Restart
    server> for a file (or uncheck and check again <menu|Follow source
    files>).
  </description>

  When a server ends by itself (for instance after a crash), the status bar
  says so, and the server is not restarted automatically: its log usually
  tells what went wrong, and <menu|Restart server> starts it again.

  <paragraph*|Information when the mouse rests>

  When the mouse rests for a moment on a word of a followed source file,
  the server is asked about it, and its answer is shown in a balloon next
  to the word, as with <key|S-F12>, if the mouse is still there. The
  balloon is closed when the mouse leaves it. This can be turned off with
  <menu|Tools|Language servers|Information on mouse rest>.

  <paragraph*|Signature help>

  After an opening bracket or a comma (the characters which the server
  asks for), the signature of the function whose arguments are being typed
  is shown on the status bar, with the current argument between
  guillemets, as in <verbatim|f (int a, \Sint b\T)>, and on the focus bar,
  with the current argument in bold. The closing bracket removes it.

  <paragraph*|The focus bar>

  In a source file which a server follows, the focus bar shows, after the
  language of the file, the name of the server (a menu, the one of
  <menu|Tools|Language servers>), whether it is starting or has stopped,
  the work it is doing (such as the indexing of the project, with its
  progress), the number of errors and warnings of the file (a click shows
  their list) and the signature which is being typed.

  With <menu|View|Interactive status bar> (in the versions of <TeXmacs>
  which have it), the status bar of a source file only shows its
  language: the menus of the fonts and the colours of the text are left
  out, since the code is coloured by its language and its font is the one
  of the preferences. The code in a document keeps them.

  <paragraph*|The list of diagnostics>

  <key|std M> opens a page with all the diagnostics of the current file, one
  per line, with its line, its column (as the server counts it: a
  character beyond U+FFFF, such as an emoji, counts twice), its severity
  and its message.
  Clicking on a line goes to the place of the diagnostic. The page is
  updated while it stays open, each time the server publishes new
  diagnostics. At most 1000 diagnostics are underlined in a file.

  <paragraph*|Formatting, renaming and quick fixes>

  <key|std F> formats the selection, or the whole file if nothing is
  selected, as the server does it (for instance with <verbatim|clang-format>
  for <name|clangd>).

  <key|std E> asks for a new name and renames the symbol at the cursor
  wherever the server finds it: in the current file and in the other files
  of the project. The files which are not open are loaded, and are then to
  be saved.

  <key|std .> asks the server for the corrections at the cursor (for instance
  \Pdid you mean ...?\Q for a misspelled name), together with the other
  changes which it proposes there (such as refactorings): a single one is
  applied at once; when there are several, they are listed on a page, from
  which one is applied by clicking on it. If the file has been edited
  since the page was opened, the change is not applied (it was made for
  the former text): ask for the corrections again. Some changes are sent
  by the server later, after a command; they are refused too when the file
  has changed meanwhile.

  <paragraph*|References and outline>

  <key|std U> lists on a page all the places where the symbol at the
  cursor is used, in the current file and in the other files of the
  project, by file, each with its line number and its line. <key|std O>
  shows the outline of the current file: its functions, classes, types,
  variables..., indented as they are nested in the file, each with its
  kind and its signature. On both pages, a click on a line goes to its
  place. The outline follows the file as long as its page stays open: it
  is made again each time the server has seen a change. At most 2000
  references are listed.

  <key|std P> asks for a name and lists the symbols of the whole project
  whose names match it (functions, types, variables...), with their files
  and lines. <key|std H> shows the calls of the function at the cursor:
  the functions which call it (a click goes to the call) and the
  functions which it calls (a click goes to their definitions). At most
  500 symbols are listed; a server which has not answered after 10
  seconds is given up (5 seconds for the requests at the cursor, such as
  the definitions and the references).

  When the cursor rests in a name, the other places of the file where the
  same symbol is used are highlighted, as the matches of a search. The
  highlights go when the cursor leaves them or when you edit. This can be
  turned off in <menu|Tools|Language servers> (the preference
  <verbatim|lsp:highlight>).

  <paragraph*|Folding>

  <key|std (> folds the block at the cursor (the body of a function, a
  class, a loop...: the blocks are those of the server): its first line
  stays, ended by an ellipsis, and its other lines are no longer shown.
  <key|std )> unfolds it; <menu|Tools|Language servers|Fold all> folds all
  the blocks, and <menu|Unfold all> shows them again.

  Folding changes only what is shown: the lines stay in the file, which is
  saved, searched, undone and given to the server as usual. The cursor
  does not stay in a folded block: moving from its first line into it
  (right at the end of the line, or down) goes to the line after it,
  moving back from the line after it (left at its start, or up) goes to
  its first line, and a block where the cursor comes otherwise (a search,
  a definition, a diagnostic) opens. A block whose first line is replaced
  or whose lines are removed or moved by an edit opens too (the blocks
  folded in it stay folded). The blocks open when the file is saved under
  another name and when the language servers are turned off.

  <paragraph*|Colours and inlay hints>

  When the server can tell the kinds of the names (<em|semantic tokens>,
  as <verbatim|clangd> does), the names of functions, of types, of macros
  and of the members of enumerations get colours of their own, also those which <TeXmacs> could not
  recognize by itself (the types and the macros which the program defines).
  The colours of the types and of the macros are those of the preferences
  of the syntax of the language; keywords, comments, strings and numbers
  keep the colours of <TeXmacs>.

  When the server gives <em|inlay hints>, they are shown in grey in the
  code: the names of the parameters before the arguments of a call
  (<verbatim|answer (x: 1)>), the types which the compiler deduces... They
  are not part of the text: the cursor goes over them, and they are not
  saved.

  The colours and the hints are those of the text which the server was
  last given: a line which you edit loses them until the server has
  answered again, a moment after you have paused. They are asked for once
  the server has published the diagnostics of the text (a server which
  publishes none gets no colours and no hints). Both can be turned off in
  <menu|Tools|Language servers> (the preferences
  <verbatim|lsp:semantic colors> and <verbatim|lsp:inlay hints>). They are
  not asked for in files of more than 20000 lines.

  <paragraph*|Incremental synchronization>

  When the server accepts it (as <name|clangd> does), only the part of a
  file which has changed is sent to it after an edit, instead of the whole
  text, which keeps large files fast.

